<Window x:Class="Nav.Viewer.Wpf.MainWindow"
        x:ClassModifier="internal"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="Nav.Viewer"
        Background="Black"
        SizeToContent="WidthAndHeight"
        ResizeMode="CanMinimize"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
        Focusable="True">
    <Grid x:Name="Chrome">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>
        <Grid.ColumnDefinitions>
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
            <ColumnDefinition Width="Auto" />
        </Grid.ColumnDefinitions>

        <!--
            The Direct3D surface. NearestNeighbor on the WPF side as well as in
            the sampler: WPF composites this image itself, and its own scaling
            would undo the point sampling if it were left to interpolate.
        -->
        <Image x:Name="Surface"
               Grid.Row="0"
               Grid.Column="0"
               Stretch="Fill"
               RenderOptions.BitmapScalingMode="NearestNeighbor"
               SnapsToDevicePixels="True" />

        <!--
            The status line is WPF chrome, not something the renderer draws.
            That decision is why IRenderer has no DrawText, and why raw Direct3D
            11, which cannot draw a glyph at all, needed no font machinery here.

            IT SPANS BOTH COLUMNS. It used to sit in column 0 alone and be sized
            to the MAP's pixel width, with the inspector's 260 points beside it
            so the line was clipped to make room for a panel it was not even
            allowed to reach under. Everything past the halfway point went: the
            tick, the run state, the pace, the selection count and every key
            hint, all of it swallowed by one ellipsis.

            WRAPPING, NOT TRIMMING, and the arithmetic is the argument: the
            guard world's line is 222 characters, which is 1464 points of
            Consolas 12, while the map and the panel together are 1240. The
            whole window is not wide enough for one line and never was, so
            trimming here could only ever mean hiding the end of it behind an
            ellipsis the reader cannot open, the same call InspectorView makes
            one column over, for the same reason.

            Wrapping is safe here for the one reason it would not be anywhere
            else: THE LINE NEVER CHANGES LENGTH. Its counters are padded to a
            constant and two tests pin it, so it always breaks in the same place
            and the bar is always the same height. A line that breathed would
            change its line count instead of its width, and shake a window sized
            to its content exactly the way the padding was added to stop.
        -->
        <Border x:Name="StatusBar"
                Grid.Row="1"
                Grid.Column="0"
                Grid.ColumnSpan="2"
                Background="Black"
                Padding="6,5">
            <TextBlock x:Name="Status"
                       Foreground="#F5F5F5"
                       FontFamily="Consolas"
                       FontSize="12"
                       TextWrapping="Wrap"
                       Text="starting..." />
        </Border>

        <!--
            The inspector, the same chrome argument as the status line one size
            up. Its width is FIXED rather than sized to its content: the window
            auto-sizes to this grid, so a panel that grew with the longest value
            in it would move the window every time a unit replanned - the same
            shake the status line's padded counters exist to prevent.

            Its height is driven from the surface in WpfHost.SizeChrome, so the
            map alone decides how tall the window is. The scroll viewer is what
            makes that safe on a small map: it takes the overflow instead of the
            window taking it.

            EMPTY IN THE XAML. What goes in this StackPanel is one heading per
            group and one row per fact, and InspectorView builds them - from
            rows nobody here has seen, whose groups and keys a source picks.
            There is nothing for a designer to lay out, and the folding and the
            tooltips both need the elements to be reachable by hand.

            IsSharedSizeScope is what keeps the key column lined up ACROSS the
            groups. Each row is its own two-column Grid so it can carry its own
            tooltip; without a shared scope those grids would each size their
            key column to their own longest key and the panel would step in and
            out down the page.
        -->
        <Border x:Name="InspectorPanel"
                Grid.Row="0"
                Grid.RowSpan="2"
                Grid.Column="1"
                Width="260"
                Background="Black"
                Padding="8,6">
            <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                          HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                          Focusable="False">
                <StackPanel x:Name="Inspector"
                            Grid.IsSharedSizeScope="True"
                            TextElement.Foreground="#F5F5F5"
                            TextElement.FontFamily="Consolas"
                            TextElement.FontSize="12" />
            </ScrollViewer>
        </Border>
    </Grid>
</Window>
